//? Title: Discrete distribution search and binary particle swarm
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

> Independent categorical and binary models

Cross-entropy refits a product of categorical distributions from the best
fraction of samples and smooths the new probabilities with the previous
ones. UMDA refits independent binary marginals from its selected subset.
PBIL updates probabilities toward the batch winner and sometimes mutates
them toward random bits. The compact GA learns from a winner–loser pair
at a time, moving each probability where the two solutions differ.

Binary GA evolves a population of bit vectors. Uniform crossover chooses
parental bits independently and mutation flips individual bits. One
elite survives each generation.

> Dependency chains and binary particle trajectories

MIMIC starts its chain at a low-entropy coordinate and adds the coordinate
with lowest entropy conditional on the previous one. Its sampler can then
express dependencies that independent marginals cannot. Binary PSO keeps
a swarm of bit vectors. Each bit is sampled from a sigmoid probability
whose logit changes according to its personal and social guides.

See [Rubinstein|https://doi.org/10.1023/A:1010091220143],
[Baluja|https://publications.ri.cmu.edu/population-based-incremental-learning-a-method-for-integrating-genetic-search-based-function-optimization-and-competitive-learning],
[Harik, Lobo and Goldberg|https://doi.org/10.1109/4235.797971],
[De Bonet, Isbell and Viola|https://papers.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/1996/hash/4c22bd444899d3b6047a10b20a2f26db-Abstract.html]
and [Kennedy and Eberhart|https://doi.org/10.1109/ICSMC.1997.637339].

> Bayesian networks and linkage trees

BOA fits a directed Bayesian network and uses the learned conditional
probabilities to generate offspring. LTGA learns a mutual-information
linkage tree and tries copying each linked subset from a paired parent.
GOMEA draws donors from the population for each subset. Both linkage
variants evaluate a changed proposal before deciding whether to keep the
transferred genes.

See [Pelikan, Goldberg and Cantú-Paz|https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=6ec551ca5b03432ce6b4795f6a99dbf074a94b17&repid=rep1&type=pdf]
and [Thierens and Bosman|https://ir.cwi.nl/pub/18916/].

> Exact binary benchmark

The 16-bit test has four independent 4-bit blocks. Each block has zero
loss at either 1100 or 0011 and otherwise costs the smaller Hamming
distance to those patterns. Thus a zero loss is known to be optimal.
Every setting uses seeds 1, 7, 42 and 123 with exactly 511 evaluations,
including duplicate points and a final partial batch or sweep.
The table reports every seed and the figure summarizes the four traces.

```{cpp, id=binary-distributions, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <array>
#include <bit>
#include <filesystem>
#include <numeric>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>

namespace o = dm::optim;

double loss(const o::CategoricalPoint& point) {
    double result = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < point.size(); i += 4) {
        const auto bits = point[i]*8 + point[i+1]*4 + point[i+2]*2 + point[i+3];
        const auto distance = std::popcount(bits ^ std::size_t{12});
        result += std::min(distance, 4 - distance);
    }
    return result;
}

int main() {
    constexpr std::size_t calls = 511;
    constexpr std::size_t dimension = 16;
    constexpr std::array<std::uint64_t, 4> seeds{1, 7, 42, 123};
    const std::vector<std::size_t> sizes(dimension, 2);
    const std::vector<std::string> labels{"Categorical CE", "UMDA", "PBIL", "Compact GA", "MIMIC", "BOA", "Binary PSO", "Binary GA", "LTGA", "GOMEA"};
    const std::array methods{o::DiscreteDistributionMethod::cross_entropy, o::DiscreteDistributionMethod::umda,
                             o::DiscreteDistributionMethod::pbil, o::DiscreteDistributionMethod::compact_ga,
                             o::DiscreteDistributionMethod::mimic, o::DiscreteDistributionMethod::boa};
    const std::vector<dm::graphics::RGBA> colors{{30,99,168}, {205,99,31}, {45,138,83},
                                                 {137,72,155}, {155,99,55}, {90,90,90}, {71,125,132}, {185,58,96},
                                                 {60,127,175}, {122,125,55}};
    std::vector<double> steps(calls);
    std::iota(steps.begin(), steps.end(), 1);
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    std::print("| Method | Seed | Calls | Incumbent loss |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < labels.size(); ++i) {
        std::vector<double> means(calls);
        for (const auto seed : seeds) {
            std::vector<double> history;
            const auto score = [&](const o::CategoricalPoint& point) {
                const auto value = loss(point);
                history.push_back(history.empty() ? value : std::min(history.back(), value));
                return value;
            };
            o::DiscreteDistributionResult result;
            if (i < methods.size()) {
                result = o::discrete_distribution_search(score, sizes, {calls, seed, methods[i], 24});
            } else if (i == methods.size()) {
                result = o::binary_particle_swarm(score, dimension, {calls, seed, 24});
            } else if (i == methods.size() + 1) {
                result = o::discrete_genetic_search(score, dimension, {calls, seed, o::GeneticEncoding::binary, 24});
            } else {
                const auto method = i == methods.size() + 2 ? o::LinkageMixingMethod::parent_pair :
                                                             o::LinkageMixingMethod::gene_pool;
                result = o::linkage_search(score, dimension, {calls, seed, 24, method});
            }
            if (history.size() != calls || result.evaluations != calls ||
                result.value != loss(result.point) || result.value != history.back()) {
                throw std::runtime_error("Inconsistent categorical search result");
            }
            for (std::size_t j = 0; j < calls; ++j) means[j] += history[j] / seeds.size();
            std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", labels[i], seed, result.evaluations, result.value);
        }
        plot.line(steps, means, labels[i], colors[i]);
    }
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::filesystem::create_directories("figures");
    const auto file = "distribution_search.svg";
    plot.title("Four complementary binary blocks").x_label("Objective calls")
        .y_label("Mean incumbent loss").legend(labels, colors).size(850, 475)
        .save_svg(std::string("build/") + file);
    std::filesystem::copy_file(std::string("build/") + file, std::string("figures/") + file,
                               std::filesystem::copy_options::overwrite_existing);
    const auto mixed = o::discrete_distribution_search(
        [](const auto& point) { return static_cast<double>((point[1] != 2) + (point[2] != 4)); },
        {1,3,5}, {120, 7});
    std::print("\nThe Cartesian product with 1, 3 and 5 choices attained loss {} in {} calls.\n",
               mixed.value, mixed.evaluations);
}
```
